&& ===========================================================================
&& L3 CATALOG ISOLATION ARM -- AIF-078 multi-workspace lifecycle, plan sec 5
&&
&& Status:  ARM / CANDIDATE. Not yet registered in cmd_regression.cpp.
&& Safety:  PRODUCTION IS READ, NEVER WRITTEN. Every mint below lands in a
&&          scratch catalog under data/tmp. Production is opened read-only
&&          through the DBF slot and closed.
&& Lane:    AIF-078 lifecycle. Prior: l0probe.dts (the lever), l1verify.dts
&&          (arms in opposite directions), workspace_purge_regression.dts
&&          (the marker discipline this arm inherits).
&&
&& WHAT THIS ARM IS FOR
&& Plan condition 2: "REGRESSION ALL leaves PRODUCTION unchanged." Today the
&& suite mints ~10 catalog rows per run into production. This arm is the
&& instrument that will say so BY FIELD VALUE, so that when L2's bracket
&& lands, "isolation works" is measured rather than believed.
&&
&& THE INSTRUMENT IS THE HIGH-WATER WS_ID, NOT A ROW COUNT.
&& Three reasons, all measured elsewhere, none guessed:
&&   1. RECCOUNT(), RECNO() and FOUND() render EMPTY in a '?' marker and STR()
&&      does not rescue them (USE_AGAIN, cited at workspace_purge_regression
&&      .dts:113-117). A marker that errors PRINTS NOTHING rather than going
&&      red, so a count-based guard would VANISH from the suite while the
&&      catalog still filled. Field comparison only.
&&   2. A mint always raises the high-water WS_ID. That is precisely the event
&&      L2 exists to contain.
&&   3. Deletes do NOT lower it -- PG_T1 proves the purge left the high-water
&&      mark alone. So this instrument answers "did anything MINT here", which
&&      is the question, and is not confused by retirement.
&&
&& A GREEN ARM THAT HAS NEVER FAILED PROVES THE SUITE RAN, NOT THAT ISOLATION
&& WORKS. So the arm proves its own detector first, in disposable space:
&& D1 mints twice into scratch and asserts the detector SEES the second mint.
&& If D1 is green and G1 is green, G1 means something. If D1 is missing or
&& false, ignore G1 entirely -- a blind detector reports peace.
&&
&& WHAT THIS ARM CANNOT DO, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT
&& It cannot verify its own restore. After SETPATH WORKSPACES is put back, no
&& expression available to a script can read the WORKSPACES slot and compare
&& it. Reading the catalog through 'SET PATH DBF workspaces' is DATA-relative
&& and therefore pinned to production whatever the WORKSPACES slot says -- the
&& exact write-here / read-there split the plan names in sec 4. So a script
&& cannot say "the catalog, wherever it currently is", and cannot confirm it
&& put the lever back. That is an argument FOR sec 4(a), made by a script that
&& hits the wall.
&&
&& KNOWN LIMIT: the scratch root is a FIXED name under the TMP slot -- as of
&& 2026-08-28 it is spelled &_TMP\wscat_l3 rather than an absolute path, so it
&& follows the slot, but two sessions running this at once would still share
&& it. The plan's per-run scratch root belongs in L2's C++ bracket, not here;
&& this arm should adopt it when it is wired INTO the suite rather than run
&& around it.
&&
&& HOW TO RUN
&&   $env:X64BASE_ALLOW_DATA = "1"
&&   .\datarun.ps1
&&   Standalone:  DOTSCRIPT scripts/l3_catalog_isolation_arm.dts
&&   Capture:     SET ALTERNATE TO <file> / SET ALTERNATE ON
&&
&& PASS CONDITION IS NOT "IT RAN". Six markers must PRINT and all six be .T.
&& A marker that errors prints nothing, so a MISSING marker is a failure even
&& though the run stayed green. Count them.
&& ===========================================================================
&& RUNTIME STATUS: GREEN 2026-08-28. 6/6 markers printed, all .T.
&& ^^ SUPERSEDED. Left verbatim. Under L2's bracket D1 went .F. --
&&    see AMENDMENT (SECOND) at the foot of this header.
&& Ran against build b30f27aa (dottalkpp v0.6).
&&
&&   L3_L0_arm_started                        .T.
&&   L3_L1_production_catalog_readable        .T.
&&   L3_D0_scratch_catalog_minted             .T.   scratch WS_ID 1
&&   L3_D1_detector_sees_a_mint               .T.   scratch WS_ID 2 > 1
&&   L3_G1_production_high_water_unchanged    .T.
&&   L3_G2_production_bottom_row_is_the_same  .T.
&&
&& TWO READINGS MATTER MORE THAN THE PASS.
&&
&& AMENDMENT 2026-08-28, AFTER A REGRESSION ALL RAN
&&
&& Reading 1 below is now FALSE and is left VERBATIM. A full REGRESSION ALL
&& ran on build 785eb9a5 and the production catalog went 252 -> 262. The
&& measurement is not rewritten because it was true, dated, and is what makes
&& the change legible; a measurement that silently retunes itself is worth
&& less than one that is superseded in the open.
&&
&& THE PLAN ESTIMATED "10 rows per REGRESSION ALL". IT IS NOW MEASURED, AND
&& THE PROVENANCE IS EXACT -- ten mints, four specs, read off the transcript:
&&
&&   WORKSPACE_SCOPE   WSPARENT 253, WSCHILD 254                    2
&&   WSMULTI           WSALPHA 255, WSBETA 256, WSGAMMA 257         3
&&   WSLADDER          WSLADR1 258, WSLADR1 259 (re-mint), WSLADR2 260   3
&&   RELSCOPE2         RSWSA 261, RSWSB 262                         2
&&                                                            total 10
&&
&& The x32 and x64 workspaces cost NOTHING -- both ADOPTED an identity the
&& catalog already held (WS_ID 214 and 207). Only NEW mints, and WSLADDER
&& mints WSLADR1 twice because WSL_T4 destroys and re-creates it to prove the
&& id moves. Every one of these specs retires what it declares, so no NAME
&& keeps a live row; the ROWS remain, which is the history D10.3 keeps.
&&
&& WHAT THIS MEANS FOR G1, AND IT IS THE POINT: the arm said its own green
&& was CHEAP because the suite had not run. The suite has now run. Bracket
&& this arm around a REGRESSION ALL today and G1/G2 GO RED -- that is the
&& demonstration plan sec 5 asked for, now available without contriving
&& anything, and it is what L2's bracket exists to turn green.
&&
&& 1. PRODUCTION IS STILL AT 252 RECORDS -- exactly the post-L1 baseline of
&&    2026-08-27. No REGRESSION ALL has run since L1. So G1's green today is
&&    cheap: production has not been touched because the suite has not run.
&&    The arm is NOT yet evidence that isolation works. It is evidence that
&&    the instrument works and that the baseline is intact. Those are
&&    different claims and only D1 currently earns its keep.
&&
&& 2. SETPATH PRINTED "warning: path does not exist" FOR A CORRECT REDIRECT.
&&    The scratch root did not exist; SETPATH accepted it non-blockingly and
&&    warned; WORKSPACE NEW then SUCCEEDED because ensure_catalog() calls
&&    fs::create_directories(catalog_dir()) (cmd_workspace.cpp). So the
&&    directory was created moments later by a different function, and the
&&    warning described a condition that was about to stop being true.
&&
&&    That is the same message the plan's L0 probe got for a MISTYPED path,
&&    where the session really did run redirected at a garbage directory. One
&&    message for "you made a typo" and for "this is a fresh scratch root and
&&    is fine" -- a warning that cannot separate a mistake from correct usage.
&&    AIF-118's shape, in the lifecycle lever itself. Recorded, not fixed.
&&
&& ===========================================================================
&& AMENDMENT 2026-08-28 (SECOND), AFTER L2's BRACKET RAN: D1 WENT FALSE.
&&
&& L2 landed and a full REGRESSION ALL ran under it. Production read 262
&& before the suite and 262 after; G1 and G2 both .T. on two separate arm
&& runs. That is the result plan condition 2 asks for. IT IS NOT CLAIMABLE,
&& because in those same two runs:
&&
&&   L3_D1_detector_sees_a_mint:.F.
&&
&& and this arm's own rule, written above and now applied to itself, says a
&& false D1 voids G1. A blind detector reports peace.
&&
&& WHY D1 WENT FALSE -- TWO HISTORIES, NOT ONE, AND THE FIXTURE CARRIED BOTH.
&& WORKSPACE NEW asks TWO authorities whether a name is taken, and they have
&& different lifetimes:
&&
&&   xbase::workspace::find_by_name_ci    RUNTIME registry, lives for the
&&     (cmd_workspace.cpp:4936)           SESSION. Refuses outright.
&&   ws_memo::ensure_durable_workspace    CATALOG on disk, lives FOREVER.
&&     (cmd_workspace.cpp:4948)           ADOPTS the existing WS_ID.
&&
&& The arm minted STABLE NAMES into a STABLE directory, so:
&&   first arm run of the session, scratch already seeded by an earlier one
&&     -> L3DET1 ADOPTED WS_ID 1. The bottom row was still the prior run's
&&        L3DET2 at 2, so scr_hw_1 read 2 and L3DET2 adopted 2 as well.
&&        scr_hw_2 > scr_hw_1 is false.
&&   second arm run in the SAME session
&&     -> "a workspace named L3DET1 already exists" -- the runtime registry
&&        refused and nothing minted at all.
&&
&& That is history-dependence wearing a green suit -- the hazard the WSLADDER
&& summary names -- committed INSIDE the arm written to detect mints. The
&& instrument was sound. The FIXTURE was dirty. Note which way this cuts: the
&& arm caught its own fixture before its green was spent on an alpha claim,
&& which is what the detector-first rule was for.
&&
&& THE FIX IS PRIOR ART, NOT A NEW IDEA. ERASE DIR was owner-ruled 2026-08-12
&& against this same failure and described in the same words
&& (cmd_erase.cpp:34-41): "the WORKSPACE WRITEBACK regression needs
&& clean-slate reruns (a leftover target makes the writeback refuse on
&& collision while the markers read the PREVIOUS run's files: a stale false
&& green)." Same shape, same lane, same remedy. So the arm now:
&&
&&   1. redirects to scratch FIRST, so every clean-up line below can only
&&      ever reach scratch;
&&   2. DESTROYs any detector workspaces an ABORTED prior run left in the
&&      runtime registry -- a clean start prints "no such workspace" twice
&&      and changes nothing;
&&   3. ERASE DIRs the scratch root. Absence is the desired end state of a
&&      teardown, so a first-ever run lands in that no-op branch by design;
&&   4. mints, and D0 now asserts scr_hw_1 = 1 EXACTLY rather than > 0. That
&&      is the pre-clean's own proof carried by the same marker: an ADOPTED
&&      id cannot be 1 on a run that just erased the row holding 1;
&&   5. DESTROYs both detector workspaces BEFORE the restore, so the next run
&&      in the SAME session mints instead of being refused.
&&
&& STEP 5 MUST STAY ABOVE THE RESTORE. WORKSPACE DESTROY WRITES: it calls
&& retire_durable_workspace before it touches the runtime registry
&& (cmd_workspace.cpp:5066). The same two lines placed after the SETPATH
&& restore would retire rows in PRODUCTION -- the arm would become the leak
&& it is watching for. Ordering is the safety argument here for the same
&& reason DESTROY makes that argument about itself.
&&
&& KNOWN LIMIT THAT REMAINS: if a run aborts between step 3 and step 5 the
&& runtime registry keeps entries whose catalog is gone; step 2 of the next
&& run cannot retire them and the mints are refused. The arm goes RED and
&& says which line refused, rather than going green. A fresh session clears
&& it. This is not fixed here because the durable fix is L2's per-run scratch
&& root, which the arm should adopt once it is wired INTO the suite.
&& ===========================================================================
&&
&& ===========================================================================
&& MEASURED 2026-08-28 AFTER THE FIXTURE FIX. THIS IS ALPHA CONDITION 2.
&&
&& One session, build fbd22307 dirty (Aug 28 2026 11:32:20), three steps:
&& arm -> REGRESSION ALL -> arm. BOTH arm runs printed 6/6 markers, all .T.
&&
&&   L3_L0_arm_started                        .T.
&&   L3_L1_production_catalog_readable        .T.   262 records
&&   L3_D0_scratch_catalog_minted             .T.   scratch WS_ID 1 EXACTLY
&&   L3_D1_detector_sees_a_mint               .T.   scratch WS_ID 2 > 1
&&   L3_G1_production_high_water_unchanged    .T.
&&   L3_G2_production_bottom_row_is_the_same  .T.   262 records
&&
&& THE DETECTOR IS ALIVE AND THE GUARD IS GREEN IN THE SAME RUN, which is the
&& pair this arm exists to produce. D1 was .F. on 2026-08-28 before the fix,
&& so the arm has now been seen BOTH WAYS -- the only way to trust a green.
&&
&& THE FIXTURE FIX BEHAVED EXACTLY AS DESIGNED, read off the transcript:
&&   WORKSPACE DESTROY: no such workspace: L3DET2     (clean start, twice)
&&   WORKSPACE DESTROY: no such workspace: L3DET1
&&   ERASE DIR: removed '...tmp/wscat_l3' (3 entrie(s)).
&&   WORKSPACE NEW: ... name L3DET1 ... WS_ID 1       (MINTED, not ADOPTED)
&&   WORKSPACE NEW: ... name L3DET2 ... WS_ID 2
&& and the teardown retired both INSIDE the redirect, so the second arm run in
&& the SAME session was neither refused nor forced to adopt. The pre-clean
&& removed 3 entries on both runs, which is the prior run's catalog going away.
&&
&& WHAT L2 DID, MEASURED BETWEEN THE TWO ARM RUNS. All four minting specs
&& bracketed, each into its own scratch root, each starting WS_IDs at 1:
&&   WORKSPACE_SCOPE  -> data/tmp/wscat_run_17
&&   WSMULTI          -> data/tmp/wscat_run_18
&&   WSLADDER         -> data/tmp/wscat_run_19
&&   RELSCOPE2        -> data/tmp/wscat_run_20
&& with a 'catalog restored to ...data/workspaces' line closing each one.
&&
&& WSLADDER IS THE SPEC THAT PROVES THE SWITCH IN 8117f4c7f WAS LOAD-BEARING:
&& it READS the catalog to assert on birth rows, and inside the bracket it read
&& the SCRATCH catalog ('SETPATH: DBF = ...tmp/wscat_run_19', 'Record count 1')
&& because it addresses that root through &_WORKSPACES rather than the pinned
&& spelling. WSL_T4 -- destroy, re-mint, id must INCREASE -- held in a catalog
&& one record deep, 1 then 2. Under the old pinned spelling this spec could not
&& have been bracketed at all.
&&
&& THE ARM STILL USES THE PINNED SPELLING ON PURPOSE and is still the only file
&& in the corpus that does. It must read PRODUCTION regardless of where the
&& WORKSPACES slot points, because that is the whole measurement.
&&
&& RESIDUE, MEASURED AND NOT SWEPT: data/tmp now holds 20 wscat_run_* roots.
&& They are the evidence of what each bracketed spec minted and they live under
&& the gitignored TMP slot. A sweep is a follow-up, deliberately not done here.
&& ===========================================================================
&&
&& ===========================================================================
&& AMENDMENT 2026-08-28 (THIRD): SET VAR CANNOT STORE A MACRO'S VALUE, AND THE
&& C++ BACKSTOP EARNED ITS KEEP ON ITS FIRST RUN.
&&
&& When this arm was wired INTO REGRESSION ALL its four hardcoded D:\code\ccode
&& paths were replaced. Three worked. The fourth did not, and the transcript
&& said so in one line:
&&
&&   SETPATH: WORKSPACES = D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\&_WORKSPACES
&&   warning: path does not exist
&&
&& The attempted spelling was 'SET VAR l3prod = &_WORKSPACES' at the top and
&& 'SETPATH WORKSPACES &l3prod' at the bottom. &l3prod EXPANDED correctly -- to
&& the literal text '&_WORKSPACES', because that is what had been STORED.
&&
&& THE CAUSE IS DISPATCH ORDER AND IT IS MEASURED, NOT SUPPOSED
&& (shell_api.cpp):
&&
&&   :329   try_handle_var_command(area, prepared)   <- VAR/SET VAR handled HERE
&&   :336   expand_macros_outside_quotes(prepared)   <- macros expanded HERE
&&
&& The VAR handler is given the RAW line and returns before the expander ever
&& runs. So the one command whose entire job is to STORE A VALUE is the one
&& command that cannot store a COMPUTED one: 'SET VAR x = &y' keeps the macro
&& TEXT. Every other command on the line gets the expansion; this one is ahead
&& of it. There is no comment at either site saying this is intended, and it
&& FAILS SILENTLY -- the assignment succeeds, and the surprise surfaces later
&& and elsewhere, wherever the text is finally used. Recorded, not fixed:
&& changing that order would change how every VAR line in the corpus parses.
&&
&& AND IT PRODUCED THE THIRD WORLD FOR SETPATH'S ONE WARNING, exactly as the
&& lifecycle plan sec 4 predicted before it had been seen: 'a genuine typo, a
&& fresh scratch root ensure_catalog() is about to create, and an unresolved
&& variable'. This is the third. One message, three conditions, one of them a
&& correct usage -- AIF-118's shape, now with a measured instance for each arm.
&&
&& WHAT SAVED THE SUITE: the arm left the WORKSPACES slot pointing at a
&& directory that does not exist, and the very next line of the transcript is
&&
&&   REGRESSION: catalog restored to D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\workspaces
&&
&& -- the WorkspacesSlotGuard the wiring commit put around this arm, doing on
&& its FIRST RUN precisely the job it was written for: a script that fails
&& between its redirect and its restore must not hand the rest of the suite a
&& moved catalog slot. It was written as a hedge against a hypothetical and was
&& cashed by the change that introduced it.
&&
&& THE MEASUREMENT ITSELF WAS NEVER IN DOUBT, and this is worth stating rather
&& than implying: L1 and G1 read production through 'SET PATH DBF workspaces',
&& which is DATA-relative and does not consult the WORKSPACES slot at all. Both
&& passes read 262. The broken line moved a LEVER, not a MEASUREMENT.
&& ===========================================================================
&& ===========================================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

&& G1 AND G2 ARE TWO INDEPENDENT FIELDS, NOT ONE CLAIM TWICE. A first draft
&& had G2 as ($prod_hw_after < $prod_hw_before + 1), which cannot disagree with
&& G1 given that the high-water mark never falls -- two markers answering one
&& question, which is the defect this project keeps finding elsewhere. G2 now
&& compares the bottom row's WS_NAME, so a mint that somehow reused an ID
&& would still move it.

&& L0 -- liveness. If this line is absent, nothing below executed.
? "L3_L0_arm_started:" + (1 = 1)

&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&& PRODUCTION BEFORE -- read only, through the DBF slot.
&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET DELETED OFF
SET PATH DBF workspaces
USE WORKSPACES
GO BOTTOM
VAR prod_hw_before = WS_ID
VAR prod_name_before = ALLTRIM(WS_NAME)
? "L3_L1_production_catalog_readable:" + ($prod_hw_before > 0)
CLOSE

&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&& DETECTOR PROOF -- both mints land in scratch. Production is not open.
&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SETPATH WORKSPACES &_TMP\wscat_l3

&& FIXTURE PRE-CLEAN -- see AMENDMENT (SECOND). The redirect above is already
&& in force, so nothing below can reach production. DESTROY comes BEFORE
&& ERASE because retiring needs the row it is retiring; on a clean start both
&& lines print "no such workspace" and change nothing.
WORKSPACE DESTROY L3DET2
WORKSPACE DESTROY L3DET1
ERASE DIR &_TMP\wscat_l3 CONFIRM

WORKSPACE NEW L3DET1
USE &_TMP\wscat_l3\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR scr_hw_1 = WS_ID
&& = 1 EXACTLY, not > 0. A fresh catalog's first mint is 1, and an ADOPTED id
&& cannot be 1 on a run that just erased the row holding 1 -- so this marker
&& is the pre-clean's proof as much as the mint's. Under the old > 0 spelling
&& an adopted 2 passed here and D1 carried the whole failure alone.
? "L3_D0_scratch_catalog_minted:" + ($scr_hw_1 = 1)
CLOSE

WORKSPACE NEW L3DET2
USE &_TMP\wscat_l3\WORKSPACES.dbf
GO BOTTOM
VAR scr_hw_2 = WS_ID
? "L3_D1_detector_sees_a_mint:" + ($scr_hw_2 > $scr_hw_1)
CLOSE

&& TEARDOWN, AND IT MUST STAY ABOVE THE RESTORE BELOW. WORKSPACE DESTROY
&& retires the durable row before it touches the runtime registry
&& (cmd_workspace.cpp:5066), so these two lines moved below the SETPATH
&& restore would write to PRODUCTION. They are here so the NEXT run in this
&& same session mints rather than being refused by the runtime registry.
WORKSPACE DESTROY L3DET2
WORKSPACE DESTROY L3DET1

&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&& RESTORE FIRST, ASSERT SECOND. The restore is unconditional and comes before
&& any further measurement, so a failure below cannot leave the lever moved.
&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&& RESTORE TO THE SAME PLACE THE READ SIDE NAMES. Both halves of this arm now
&& resolve production through the DATA root: the reads use the pinned
&& DATA-relative 'SET PATH DBF workspaces' and this puts the WORKSPACES slot
&& back to the same directory, so read and restore AGREE BY CONSTRUCTION rather
&& than by two people keeping one path in their heads.
&&
&& A capture-and-restore was tried first and was WRONG TWICE. It did not work
&& (see AMENDMENT THIRD), and even had it worked it would have restored
&& "wherever the slot pointed on entry", which is not necessarily the catalog
&& this arm just MEASURED -- two authorities for one location, in the file
&& whose whole subject is which catalog got written.
SETPATH WORKSPACES &_DATA\workspaces

&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&& PRODUCTION AFTER -- the claim.
&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET PATH DBF workspaces
USE WORKSPACES
GO BOTTOM
VAR prod_hw_after = WS_ID
? "L3_G1_production_high_water_unchanged:" + ($prod_hw_after = $prod_hw_before)
? "L3_G2_production_bottom_row_is_the_same_row:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = $prod_name_before)
CLOSE
SET DELETED ON

&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
&& HOW TO SEE THIS ARM GO RED, which is the only way to trust it green:
&&
&&   1. Run this arm.                          G1, G2 expected .T.
&&   2. At the prompt, WORKSPACE NEW REDPROOF  (mints into PRODUCTION)
&&   3. Run this arm again.                    G1, G2 must go .F.
&&   4. WORKSPACE DESTROY REDPROOF then
&&      WORKSPACE DELETE REDPROOF              retires the row; the high-water
&&                                             mark stays raised BY DESIGN
&&                                             (PG_T1), so step 1's baseline
&&                                             does not come back. That is the
&&                                             instrument behaving correctly,
&&                                             not a leak.
&&
&& Step 2 WRITES ONE ROW TO THE PRODUCTION CATALOG and is therefore NOT part
&& of this script. It needs an explicit go, exactly like L1 did.
&& ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
